l)** and **UDP (User Datagram Protocol)**, focusing on guarantees, typical use cases, and behavior during packet loss.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Core Guarantees\n\n| Feature | TCP (Transmission Control Protocol) | UDP (User Datagram Protocol) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Connection Type** | **Connection-oriented**: Requires a handshake (SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK) before data transfer. | **Connectionless**: No handshake; data is sent immediately. |\n| **Reliability** | **Guaranteed delivery**: Ensures all packets arrive in order. Uses acknowledgments (ACKs) and retransmissions. | **Best-effort delivery**: No guarantee that packets will arrive, arrive in order, or not be duplicated. |\n| **Ordering** | **Ordered**: Data is reassembled in the correct sequence at the receiver. | **Unordered**: Packets may arrive out of order or not at all. |\n| **Flow Control** | **Yes**: Uses sliding window mechanisms to prevent overwhelming the receiver. | **No**: The sender transmits at whatever rate it wants. |\n| **Congestion Control", "client": {"ttft_s": 0.3440291000006255, "decode_s": 2.115909300002386, "total_s": 2.460142800002359, "n_gen": 256, "n_chunks": 256, "tg": 120.51556274161301, "pp": 87.2019256508983}}
